THE AI-NATIVE BIOLOGICAL DISCOVERY INSTITUTION

A FORMAL THEORY OF INTEGRATIVE EPISTEMIC ORGANIZATIONS FOR THE AGE OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE


Author: Ouadi Maakoul
AI Collaborators: Claude (Anthropic), ChatGPT (OpenAI), Gemini (Google), DeepSeek

Date: June 2026



## ABSTRACT

The capacity of human civilization to generate biological knowledge has outpaced its capacity to integrate it. This dissertation proposes and formally develops the AI-Native Biological Discovery Institution (ABDI) as a solution to this integration crisis, advancing the theoretical claim that the fragmentation of biological knowledge is not an incidental organizational failure but a necessary structural consequence of institutional architectures designed for a data-scarce world that no longer exists. We introduce thirty formal definitions and nine theorems, together with one corollary, constituting a novel theoretical framework for integrative epistemic organizations at planetary scale.

The central theoretical contributions are as follows. First, we prove the Fragmentation Theorem, establishing that the epistemic fragmentation index of any traditional scientific institution grows toward unity with literature volume, rendering knowledge integration computationally intractable beyond a critical threshold for any institution bounded by human cognitive capacity. Second, we develop a formal specification of the Global Biological Knowledge Graph (GBKG) as a typed, confidence-weighted, contradiction-visible hypergraph, and prove that Bayesian confidence propagation over this structure converges almost surely to a stable knowledge state approximating biological truth, under specified regularity conditions. Third, we introduce the formal definition of an AI Scientist as a confidence-generating epistemic agent and prove the Complementarity Theorem, demonstrating that human-AI epistemic coupling achieves discovery rates structurally exceeding either agent class in isolation, with the advantage growing as the formal-computational component of biological discovery expands. Fourth, we formalize the multiscale biological digital twin as a hierarchy of coupled, locally identifiable modules and prove that global identifiability follows from local identifiability under modular composition. Fifth, we prove the Integration Sufficiency Theorem, establishing the minimal architectural conditions for overcoming epistemic fragmentation at planetary scale, and demonstrate by explicit construction that ABDI instantiates precisely these conditions.

The dissertation is grounded in a comprehensive review of AI-native biological research conducted in June 2026, confirming that the individual components of ABDI—knowledge graphs, AI scientists, autonomous laboratories, digital twins—are under active development by leading global institutions. The theoretical contribution lies in proving why these components must be integrated into a unified architecture to achieve planetary-scale biological discovery, and in providing the formal framework for that integration.

