## CHAPTER 7: GOVERNANCE THEORY FOR GLOBAL EPISTEMIC INSTITUTIONS

### 7.1 Tiered Access as Mechanism Design

Governance of ABDI requires solving a mechanism design problem: how to structure incentives and access rules such that diverse participants—academic researchers, pharmaceutical companies, national governments, international health organizations—are induced to contribute data and expertise to the global knowledge graph while protecting legitimate proprietary, privacy, and security interests.

**Definition 29 (Tiered Access Function).** Define a tiered access function A: V × E × C_P → {Open, Controlled, Closed} where V is the set of claim nodes, E is the edge set, and C_P is the set of participant categories. The Open tier comprises validated claims, methodological provenance, and confidence scores, publicly accessible without registration. The Controlled tier comprises individual-level human genomic data, dual-use pathogen sequences, and AI model weights capable of synthesizing dangerous biological agents, accessible only to vetted participants under governance board authorization. The Closed tier comprises proprietary pharmaceutical data contributed by industry partners, used internally for hypothesis generation but not publicly disclosed. The tiered access function must satisfy two fairness conditions: (FC1) no participant who contributes data to tier k gains access to other participants' data in the same tier without reciprocal agreement; and (FC2) the Open tier grows monotonically over time as proprietary claims are validated and intellectual property protections expire.

### 7.2 Dual-Use Risk Formalism

**Definition 30 (Dual-Use Risk Index).** For a hypothesis h ∈ Σ, define the dual-use risk index R(h) ∈ [0,1] as the estimated probability that h, if true and widely known, could be operationalized to cause significant harm by a malicious actor without requiring exceptional resources or technical expertise. The dual-use risk index is estimated by a specialized risk assessment module trained on biosecurity domain expertise and updated continuously by the governance board.

High-R(h) hypotheses above threshold ρ trigger automatic restriction to the Controlled tier and mandatory ethics board review before any experimental execution. AI Scientists are constitutively incapable of designing experiments for hypotheses with R(h) above an absolute threshold ρ* > ρ without explicit governance board authorization, implementing dual-use governance at the level of the discovery pipeline rather than as a post-hoc review mechanism.

The Pandemic Defense division presents the sharpest governance challenge: the hypotheses with highest epidemiological value—concerning novel pathogen transmissibility and virulence mechanisms—are precisely the hypotheses with highest dual-use risk. Resolving this tension requires the tiered access framework to operate at the level of individual hypotheses with rapid governance review cycles compatible with pandemic response timescales, and mandates international representation on review boards to prevent single-jurisdiction capture.

### 7.3 Global Participation Under Regulatory Heterogeneity

Achieving genuine global participation across jurisdictions with incompatible regulatory frameworks is among the most serious practical challenges for ABDI. Data protection regimes, intellectual property frameworks, and dual-use research regulations vary substantially across jurisdictions. We propose a Regulatory Envelope Framework: ABDI defines the maximal set of knowledge-sharing activities permissible under the intersection of all participating jurisdictions' regulations. Activities outside this intersection are performed only in jurisdictions where they are explicitly permitted, with federated architecture ensuring that only outputs satisfying all applicable jurisdictions' requirements are integrated into the global knowledge graph. This framework permits progressive expansion of permissible activities as regulatory harmonization develops, rather than requiring harmonization as a precondition for participation.

