APPENDIX B: PROOFS OF THEOREMS AND COROLLARY

B.1 Theorem 1: Contradiction Visibility Necessity

Statement. Let K be any knowledge representation system for biological claims that does not represent contradictions as first-class objects with severity scores. Then the expected epistemic cost of undetected contradictions—the expected number of false downstream inferences derived from conflicting claims without awareness of the conflict—grows as \Omega(n^2) in the number of claims n.

Proof. Let the system contain n claim nodes. A contradiction exists whenever two claims v_i, v_j are logically inconsistent under the same context. Let \delta \in (0,1] be the contradiction density, i.e., the fraction of all unordered claim pairs that are in the contradiction relation C. The total number of contradicting pairs is \binom{n}{2} \delta = \frac{\delta}{2} n(n-1) = \Theta(n^2).

Without explicit contradiction representation, each such pair is invisible to the system’s inference engine. Consider a single undetected contradiction (v_i, v_j). In a knowledge graph with maximum out-degree d (bounded, as biological networks are sparse), each claim may support up to d direct downstream inferences. These in turn support further inferences, forming a directed acyclic inference subgraph. Let the average depth of such inference chains be k. Then a single undetected contradiction can cause at most d^k false downstream inferences (treating the inference structure as a d-ary tree of depth k). For a conservative bound, note that biological inference rarely exceeds depth k=5 in practice, and d is typically on the order of 10–100. Hence each undetected contradiction yields at most O(1) false inferences (a constant dependent on the network structure, but bounded independently of n).

Now, the expected number of false inferences contributed by all undetected contradictions is the product of the number of contradicting pairs and the average number of false inferences per pair. Since the number of contradicting pairs is \Theta(n^2), the expected total false inferences is \Theta(n^2 \cdot \text{avg. false inferences per contradiction}). Because the average false inferences per contradiction is bounded below by a positive constant (at least 1, since the very fact that two conflicting claims coexist means that a reasoner might use both to draw a false conclusion), the total cost is \Omega(n^2).

More formally, define the epistemic cost as the expected number of unique false triples (v_i, v_j, w) where w is a downstream node derived by combining v_i and v_j without awareness of their inconsistency. Under a random graph model with constant edge probability p, the number of nodes that can be reached via a path from both v_i and v_j (i.e., nodes that could be inferred using both) is of order O(1) on average. So each contradicting pair contributes at least one expected false inference. Thus expected cost = \delta \binom{n}{2} \cdot c \ge c' n^2 for sufficiently large n. This completes the proof. \square

