B.2 Theorem 2: Confidence Propagation Convergence

Statement. Let hypothesis h receive a sequence of experimental evidence items e_1, e_2, \dots, e_t, each conditionally independent given h. Suppose the likelihood ratios L_t = P(e_t \mid h) / P(e_t \mid \neg h) satisfy |L_t - 1| \ge \varepsilon > 0 for all t, and that the evidence items are identically distributed conditional on h. Then the Bayesian confidence score \alpha_t = \alpha(h \mid e_1,\dots,e_t) converges almost surely to 1 if h is true, and to 0 if h is false, as t \to \infty.

Proof. We work with the log-odds (logit) of the confidence. Define

\ell_0 = \log \frac{\alpha_0}{1 - \alpha_0}, \qquad
\ell_t = \log \frac{\alpha_t}{1 - \alpha_t},

where \alpha_0 is the prior confidence. The Bayesian update rule (Definition 11) implies

\ell_t = \ell_0 + \sum_{s=1}^t \log L_s.

If h is true, then for each s, the random variable \log L_s has a positive expectation. Indeed, because the true distribution of the evidence is P(\cdot \mid h), and because the likelihood ratio L_s satisfies E_{h}[\log L_s] = D_{\text{KL}}(P(\cdot \mid h) \,\|\, P(\cdot \mid \neg h)) > 0 (the Kullback-Leibler divergence is strictly positive when the distributions are not identical). The condition |L_t - 1| \ge \varepsilon ensures that the KL divergence is bounded away from zero. Moreover, the log-likelihood ratios are bounded random variables because the evidence space is finite (experimental outcomes belong to a finite set of possible results) and L_t is bounded away from 0 and \infty by the assumption that likelihoods are non-degenerate. Hence \log L_s has finite variance.

By the strong law of large numbers for i.i.d. sequences (or for martingale difference sequences if we relax identical distribution but keep bounded second moments), the average \frac{1}{t} \sum_{s=1}^t \log L_s converges almost surely to the expected value \mu = E_{h}[\log L_1] > 0. Consequently, \ell_t = \ell_0 + t \cdot \frac{1}{t} \sum \log L_s \to +\infty almost surely. Therefore \alpha_t = e^{\ell_t}/(1+e^{\ell_t}) \to 1.

If h is false, the true distribution is P(\cdot \mid \neg h). Then E_{\neg h}[\log L_s] = -D_{\text{KL}}(P(\cdot \mid \neg h) \,\|\, P(\cdot \mid h)) < 0. By the same law of large numbers, \ell_t \to -\infty a.s., so \alpha_t \to 0.

Thus, under the stated conditions, confidence converges almost surely to the correct truth value. \square

