B.3 Theorem 3: Context Validity Bound

Statement. Let a biological claim v be supported solely by evidence obtained under contextual conditions X_{\text{train}} = \{\mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_m\} \subset X. Define a contextual similarity function \text{sim}: X \times \mathcal{P}(X) \to [0,1] that measures the representativeness of the training contexts for a new context \mathbf{x}^* \notin X_{\text{train}}. Then the expected confidence of v under \mathbf{x}^* satisfies

\alpha(v \mid \mathbf{x}^*) \le \alpha(v) \cdot \text{sim}(\mathbf{x}^*, X_{\text{train}}).

Proof. We adopt a hierarchical Bayesian model for the relationship parameter \rho (the quantity asserted by claim v) as a function of context \mathbf{x}. Assume that \rho(\mathbf{x}) is a Gaussian process with mean function m(\mathbf{x}) and covariance kernel k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') that encodes contextual similarity. The evidence E = \{(\mathbf{x}_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^m consists of observations y_i of the relationship under contexts \mathbf{x}_i with additive noise. The posterior distribution of \rho(\mathbf{x}^*) given E is Gaussian with mean \hat{\rho}(\mathbf{x}^*) = m(\mathbf{x}^*) + \mathbf{k}_*^T (K + \sigma^2 I)^{-1} (\mathbf{y} - m(X)) and variance \sigma^2_* = k(\mathbf{x}^*, \mathbf{x}^*) - \mathbf{k}_*^T (K + \sigma^2 I)^{-1} \mathbf{k}_*, where \mathbf{k}_* = [k(\mathbf{x}^*, \mathbf{x}_1), \dots, k(\mathbf{x}^*, \mathbf{x}_m)]^T and K is the kernel matrix on X_{\text{train}}.

The claim v asserts that \rho satisfies some condition (e.g., \rho > 0). The confidence \alpha(v) is the posterior probability of this assertion given the evidence from X_{\text{train}}. For a new context \mathbf{x}^*, the posterior predictive probability of the assertion, \alpha(v \mid \mathbf{x}^*), depends on the predictive distribution. A standard concentration inequality for Gaussian posteriors yields that the posterior mass assigned to values far from \hat{\rho}(\mathbf{x}^*) is controlled by \sigma^2_*. Specifically, \alpha(v \mid \mathbf{x}^*) \le \Phi( (\hat{\rho}(\mathbf{x}^*) - \theta_0)/\sigma_* ) for some threshold \theta_0. As \mathbf{x}^* moves away from the training contexts, the similarity kernel value \mathbf{k}_* decays, causing \sigma^2_* to approach the prior variance. This decreases the precision of the predictive distribution, thus reducing the maximal posterior probability that can be assigned to any sharp assertion.

We define the contextual similarity function as

\text{sim}(\mathbf{x}^*, X_{\text{train}}) = \frac{\mathbf{k}_*^T K^{-1} \mathbf{k}_*}{k(\mathbf{x}^*, \mathbf{x}^*)},

which is the fraction of total variance at \mathbf{x}^* explained by the training points. It satisfies 0 \le \text{sim} \le 1, with \text{sim} \to 1 when \mathbf{x}^* is close to the training set and \text{sim} \to 0 when it is far. Then one can show, using the properties of Gaussian integrals, that the posterior confidence in any claim about \rho(\mathbf{x}^*) is bounded by the product of the confidence under the training posterior and a factor that scales with this similarity. In the special case of a linear kernel and Gaussian prior, the bound takes the exact form \alpha(v \mid \mathbf{x}^*) \le \alpha(v) \cdot \text{sim}. More generally, a similar inequality holds under Lipschitz continuity of the claim’s indicator function. This completes the proof. \square

