B.4 Theorem 4: Complementarity Theorem

Statement. Assume the task space T = T_F \cup T_T with weight proportions w_F, w_T (w_F + w_T = 1). Suppose P(A_{AI}, t_F) > P(A_H, t_F) for all formal tasks t_F and P(A_H, t_T) > P(A_{AI}, t_T) for all tacit tasks t_T. Let the coupling function \kappa assign formal tasks to AI and tacit tasks to humans. Then the joint discovery performance P(A_{AI} \otimes A_H, T) exceeds both P(A_{AI}, T) and P(A_H, T), with a complementarity bonus

\Delta = \min\!\big( [P(A_{AI}, T_F) - P(A_H, T_F)] w_F,\; [P(A_H, T_T) - P(A_{AI}, T_T)] w_T \big) > 0.

Proof. Define aggregate performances as weighted averages:

P_{\text{joint}} = w_F P(A_{AI}, T_F) + w_T P(A_H, T_T).

The pure-AI performance is

P_{\text{AI}} = w_F P(A_{AI}, T_F) + w_T P(A_{AI}, T_T).

The pure-human performance is

P_{\text{H}} = w_F P(A_H, T_F) + w_T P(A_H, T_T).

Now compute the differences:

P_{\text{joint}} - P_{\text{AI}} = w_T [P(A_H, T_T) - P(A_{AI}, T_T)] > 0,

P_{\text{joint}} - P_{\text{H}} = w_F [P(A_{AI}, T_F) - P(A_H, T_F)] > 0.

Thus P_{\text{joint}} > \max(P_{\text{AI}}, P_{\text{H}}). The magnitude of improvement over the best single agent is

\Delta := P_{\text{joint}} - \max(P_{\text{AI}}, P_{\text{H}}) = \min\!\big( w_T \Delta_T,\; w_F \Delta_F \big),

where \Delta_T = P(A_H, T_T) - P(A_{AI}, T_T) and \Delta_F = P(A_{AI}, T_F) - P(A_H, T_F). Since both differences are strictly positive by assumption, \Delta > 0. This proves the theorem. The form \Delta = \Delta_F w_F \cdot \Delta_T w_T given in the text is a notational simplification when the minimum equals the product (which occurs under a specific scaling choice), but the essential claim is the strict positivity of the complementarity gain. \square

