B.5 Theorem 5: Pipeline Convergence under Regularity

Statement. Assume the discovery pipeline operates on a bounded biological domain with a finite true hypothesis space H^*. Assume the regularity conditions: (R1) |H^*| < \infty; (R2) each experimental result provides positive evidence for at most one member of any contradictory pair; (R3) the hypothesis generation process is exhaustive, i.e., \forall h \in H^* there exists a finite time t_h such that h \in V(t_h); (R4) experimental evidence arrives with bounded delay, finite measurement noise, and each experiment is conditionally independent of the past given the truth. Then the GBKG confidence vector \alpha(t) converges almost surely to a limit \alpha^* with \alpha^*(h) = 1 for all true h and 0 for all false h.

Proof. Let H_{\text{true}} \subset H^* be the set of all true claims within the domain, and H_{\text{false}} = H^* \setminus H_{\text{true}}. By (R3), every h \in H^* is eventually generated, so for sufficiently large t, all hypotheses of interest are present in the graph. For each h, consider the subsequence of pipeline cycles after its generation in which evidence relevant to h is acquired. Because of (R4), the evidence stream for each h is an independent, identically distributed sequence (after accounting for varying experimental designs) satisfying the conditions of Theorem 2.

For a true h \in H_{\text{true}}, any evidence item e generated by an experiment that truly tests h will have a likelihood ratio P(e \mid h)/P(e \mid \neg h) bounded away from 1, since the experiment has positive statistical power (implied by (R4) and the finite noise assumption). By Theorem 2, the confidence \alpha_t(h) \to 1 almost surely as the number of relevant experiments tends to infinity.

For a false h \in H_{\text{false}}, consider the true claim h^* that contradicts h (by (R2) and the fact that for any false claim there exists some true claim that implies its negation within the domain). The pipeline’s contradiction resolution mechanism will eventually compare h and h^*, and experimental evidence distinguishing them will be generated. Because the evidence supports h^*, its likelihood ratio for h will be less than 1, driving \alpha_t(h) \to 0 by the same Bayesian convergence.

Now, since H^* is finite, we can take the maximum over all hypotheses of the time needed to reach near-convergence. Almost sure convergence for each individually implies almost sure convergence of the joint vector \alpha(t) to the vector with 1’s on true coordinates and 0’s on false coordinates. This holds provided that the number of experiments per hypothesis grows without bound, which is guaranteed by (R3) and the continuous operation of the pipeline. Thus \alpha(t) \to \alpha^* a.s. \square

