B.6 Theorem 6: Perturbation Resilience

Statement. Under the tiered evidence weighting system, a fraudulent claim v_f injected with initial confidence \alpha_0 will be reduced to \alpha(v_f) < \varepsilon within T pipeline cycles, where T = O\big( \frac{1}{\rho} \log \frac{\alpha_0}{(1-\alpha_0)\varepsilon} \big) and \rho is the replication monitoring rate.

Proof. The fraudulent claim is false, i.e., \neg v_f is true. In each cycle, the replication monitoring system attempts an independent replication of the claim with probability \rho. When a replication experiment is conducted, it generates evidence e with a likelihood ratio L = P(e \mid v_f)/P(e \mid \neg v_f) < 1. Because the experiment is a well-designed replication, the likelihood ratio satisfies L \le \lambda < 1 for some fixed \lambda (e.g., under a standard power analysis). Let \gamma = -\log \lambda > 0.

Let \ell_t be the log-odds after t cycles. Initially \ell_0 = \log \frac{\alpha_0}{1-\alpha_0}. Each replication attempt, occurring independently with probability \rho, adds \log L to \ell_t. Thus the expected drift per cycle is -\rho \gamma. By the Chernoff bound for sums of i.i.d. increments, the actual sum after t cycles concentrates around -t \rho \gamma. To guarantee \alpha_t < \varepsilon, we need \ell_t \le \log \frac{\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon}. Starting from \ell_0, we require a total negative drift of at least \ell_0 - \log \frac{\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon}. So the number of cycles T must satisfy

T \rho \gamma \ge \ell_0 - \log \frac{\varepsilon}{1-\varepsilon} \quad \Longrightarrow \quad T \ge \frac{ \log \frac{\alpha_0}{(1-\alpha_0)\varepsilon} }{ \rho \gamma }.

The right-hand side grows logarithmically in 1/\varepsilon and inversely with \rho. Thus T = O(\frac{1}{\rho} \log \frac{1}{\varepsilon}). \square

