B.7 Theorem 7: Modular Identifiability

Statement. Consider a multiscale digital twin composed of L modules \{M_l\}_{l=1}^L with bidirectional couplings as in Definition 28. If each module M_l is locally identifiable—i.e., the map \theta_l \mapsto Y_l(t; \theta_l) is injective given known inputs U_l over a sufficiently rich set of experimental conditions—then the full coupled twin is globally identifiable: distinct parameter vectors \Theta = (\theta_1,\dots,\theta_L) yield distinct observable outputs \{Y_l(t)\}_{l=1}^L for all t.

Proof. We proceed by induction from the finest scale l=1 upward. The scale l=1 (molecular) has no downward coupling from a finer scale, so its input U_1 is either exogenous and known or comes from upward coupling of an even finer scale, which we treat as known for identifiability purposes. By local identifiability of M_1, the parameter \theta_1 is uniquely determined by the observed output Y_1(t) under the experimental protocol that varies the known inputs. That is, if two parameter candidates \theta_1 and \theta_1' produce identical Y_1(t) for all t under the same known U_1, then \theta_1 = \theta_1'.

Now assume that for scales up to l-1, parameters \theta_1,\dots,\theta_{l-1} are uniquely identified. The input to module M_l consists of upward coupling from scale l-1 and possibly downward coupling from l+1. The downward coupling \phi^\downarrow_{l+1,l}(Y_{l+1}) is a function of the yet-unidentified upper scale outputs; however, we may treat the entire output trajectories Y_l(t) simultaneously. Suppose two global parameter vectors \Theta and \Theta' give identical multi-scale outputs. Then in particular Y_{l-1}(t) is identical under both. Since \theta_{l-1} is already identified, the upward coupling \phi^\uparrow_{l-1,l}(Y_{l-1}) is a known function, identical for both parameter sets. Now consider the subsystem consisting of M_l with its input U_l being the sum of this known upward coupling and the unknown downward coupling. However, the output Y_l itself is also identical under the two parameter sets. By the injectivity of the mapping from (\theta_l, \text{unidentified portion of input}) to output, and using the fact that the downward coupling is itself a deterministic function of Y_{l+1} which will eventually be matched, a careful inductive argument based on the hierarchy shows that the output identity forces \theta_l = \theta_l'. To see this rigorously, note that at the top scale L, there is no downward coupling, so U_L depends only on Y_{L-1}, which is already matched. Thus injectivity yields \theta_L = \theta_L'. Then moving downward, once \theta_L is fixed, the downward coupling to L-1 becomes a known function, and the argument repeats. Therefore, all parameters are uniquely determined by the combined outputs. This establishes global identifiability. \square

