B.8 Theorem 8: The Fragmentation Theorem

Statement. Let a scientific institution I consist of m agents, each with bounded active cognitive capacity K_{\text{max}} (the maximum number of claims they can actively process simultaneously). Assume that (A2) productive integration of a claim pair (c_i, c_j) requires both claims to be within the same agent’s active set; and (A3) there is no systematic cross-agent integration mechanism beyond informal collaboration, which expands effective reach by a factor s (social network size). For a literature of n claims, the epistemic fragmentation index F(I, n) satisfies

F(I, n) \ge 1 - \frac{m (s K_{\text{max}})^2}{n^2}.

Consequently, for any fixed m, K_{\text{max}}, s, we have F(I, n) \to 1 as n \to \infty.

Proof. A “productive connection” is a pair of claims whose joint consideration yields a novel hypothesis or resolves a contradiction. There are \binom{n}{2} = \frac{n(n-1)}{2} such pairs in total.

Each agent can actively hold at most K_{\text{max}} claims. Through informal collaboration (discussions, conferences, etc.), the agent may indirectly access claims held by a social neighborhood of size s, effectively expanding their reachable set to at most s K_{\text{max}} unique claims. Thus a single agent can actively integrate at most \binom{s K_{\text{max}}}{2} pairs.

With m agents, assuming worst-case overlap (the bound is most conservative when the sets of reachable claims are disjoint, maximizing total coverage), the total number of pairs that can be integrated by some agent is at most

m \cdot \binom{s K_{\text{max}}}{2} \le m \frac{(s K_{\text{max}})^2}{2}.

Therefore, the fraction of all possible pairs that are discoverable is at most

\frac{ m (s K_{\text{max}})^2 / 2 }{ n(n-1)/2 } = \frac{ m (s K_{\text{max}})^2 }{ n(n-1) } \le \frac{ m (s K_{\text{max}})^2 }{ n^2 } \quad \text{for } n \ge 2.

The fragmentation index F is the fraction of productive connections not discoverable, so

F \ge 1 - \frac{ m (s K_{\text{max}})^2 }{ n^2 }.

As n \to \infty, the subtracted term tends to zero, so F \to 1. This completes the proof. \square

