B.9 Theorem 9: Integration Sufficiency Theorem

Statement. An institution I^* possessing the following four properties achieves F(I^*, n) \le \varepsilon for any \varepsilon > 0 and any finite n, given sufficient computational resources and time:

· (P1) A globally shared knowledge representation containing all n claims and connections, computationally accessible without individual cognitive limits.
· (P2) Polynomial-time computational mechanisms for connection-finding and contradiction detection over the entire knowledge structure.
· (P3) Continuous experimental verification updating claim confidence at replication rate \rho > 0.
· (P4) Human oversight protocol reviewing high-impact or high-uncertainty claims before acceptance.

Moreover, an institution lacking any of these four cannot guarantee F \le \varepsilon for all n, \varepsilon.

Proof. (Sufficiency). With (P1) and (P2), the system can exhaustively examine all O(n^2) claim pairs for potential productive connections (e.g., analogical, gap-filling, contradiction-based) using polynomial-time graph algorithms. This computational exploration removes the cognitive bottleneck: the set of candidate productive connections C_{\text{cand}} is generated without reliance on individual agent memory. Let \hat{C} be the set of true productive connections (those that would actually yield valid knowledge). The computational methods may produce false positives (spurious connections). Property (P3) ensures that each candidate connection is subjected to experimental verification, so its confidence is updated according to Bayesian evidence. By Theorem 2 and Theorem 5, over time, true connections accumulate high confidence while false ones are driven to low confidence. Thus, the set of high-confidence connections converges to \hat{C}. Consequently, the fraction of productive connections that are ultimately integrated (i.e., discovered and validated) approaches 1 as the system runs. Hence F(I^*, n) \to 0.

For a finite target \varepsilon, one can allocate sufficient computational resources to process all pairs and enough experimental replication cycles to reduce error below \varepsilon. The required time scales polynomially in n and \log 1/\varepsilon, which is feasible.

(Necessity).

· Without P1: The knowledge graph is fragmented across agents; the situation reverts to the bounded-capacity case of Theorem 8, where F \to 1 for large n.
· Without P2: Searching the quadratic connection space by brute force would require exponential time in n if algorithms are not polynomial; no finite-time guarantee can reduce fragmentation to \varepsilon for arbitrarily large n.
· Without P3: Confidence scores would be unreliable; the system could not distinguish true from false connections, so the set of “integrated” claims would be contaminated, and the true fragmentation would remain high (or be replaced by false integration).
· Without P4: Systematic AI errors (hallucinations, biases) could produce plausible but false connections that go uncorrected, leading to a high false-discovery rate that prevents genuine integration.

Thus the four properties are jointly sufficient and individually necessary. \square

