B.10 Corollary 1: ABDI Convergence Corollary

Statement. Under the regularity conditions of Theorems 5 and 7, the GBKG confidence vector converges almost surely to the true biological knowledge landscape within any bounded domain. Moreover, ABDI’s fragmentation index satisfies F(\text{ABDI}, n) \to 0 as computational resources are scaled proportionally with n.

Proof. ABDI instantiates all four properties of Theorem 9 (as demonstrated in §8.3). Therefore, by Theorem 9, its fragmentation index can be made arbitrarily small for any finite n given sufficient resources. The almost-sure convergence of confidence scores to the true knowledge landscape is guaranteed by Theorem 5, which applies because the ABDI pipeline satisfies the regularity conditions (bounded domain, exhaustive hypothesis generation via AI Scientists, continuous experimentation, and independent evidence). The modular identifiability theorem (Theorem 7) ensures that the digital twin component, used to simulate and predict across scales, does not introduce unidentifiable parameters that would corrupt the knowledge graph. Hence, the combined result holds. \square

